}}{{Politics of São Tomé and Príncipe}}

Presidential elections were held in [[São Tomé and Príncipe]] in 2011, the first round beginning on 17 July 2011 with a [[Run-off election|run-off]] held on 7 August 2011. Incumbent [[List of heads of government of São Tomé and Príncipe|President]] [[Fradique de Menezes]] had served the maximum two terms and could not constitutionally seek a third term.<ref name="voa">{{Cite news|author=Staff writers|title=Sao Tome Prepares for Presidential Run-Off|url=http://blogs.voanews.com/breaking-news/2011/08/06/sao-tome-prepares-for-presidential-run-off/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120315190403/http://blogs.voanews.com/breaking-news/2011/08/06/sao-tome-prepares-for-presidential-run-off/|url-status=dead|archive-date=15 March 2012|date=6 August 2011|newspaper=Voice of America|access-date=7 August 2011}}</ref><ref name="veiga1">{{Cite news|author=Veiga, Abel|title=Sao Tome presidential vote set for run-off|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5it3oZM-oZ0b8GRA5guc0QbrEmylA|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130103023803/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5it3oZM-oZ0b8GRA5guc0QbrEmylA|url-status=dead|archive-date=3 January 2013|newspaper=Google News|access-date=20 July 2011}}</ref> The final result saw former president [[Manuel Pinto da Costa]], aged 74, elected in a narrow victory against Speaker of Parliament [[Evaristo Carvalho]].<ref name="publico">{{Cite news|author=Agence France-Presse|title=Manuel Pinto da Costa eleito Presidente de São Tomé|url=http://www.publico.pt/Mundo/manuel-pinto-da-costa-eleito-presidente-de-sao-tome_1506653|date=8 August 2011|newspaper=Público|language=Portuguese|access-date=7 August 2011|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120307170112/http://www.publico.pt/Mundo/manuel-pinto-da-costa-eleito-presidente-de-sao-tome_1506653|archivedate=7 March 2012}}</ref>

The first round was contested by approximately 120 candidates.<ref name="veiga1"/> The candidate from President de Menezes' party, [[Force for Change Democratic Movement – Liberal Party|Force for Change Democratic Movement–Liberal Party]] ({{langx|pt|Movimento Democrático das Forças de Mudança–Partido Liberal}}, MDFM–PL), was Delfim Neves, who jointly represented the MDFM–PL and his own [[Democratic Convergence Party – Reflection Group|Democratic Convergence Party]] ({{langx|pt|Partido de Convergencia Democratica}}, PCD–GR). Pinto da Costa, who ran [[Independent (politics)|independently]],<ref>{{Cite web|author=Pinto da Costa, Manuel|title=Homepage|url=http://www.presidentepintodacosta.com|access-date=23 July 2011|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120327194408/http://www.presidentepintodacosta.com/|archivedate=27 March 2012}}</ref> won the most votes but failed to receive the majority required to claim an outright victory. Carvalho, of the ruling party [[Independent Democratic Action]] ({{langx|pt|Acção Democratica Independente}}, ADI), a former prime minister and the incumbent Speaker of the [[National Assembly of São Tomé and Príncipe|National Assembly]], placed second.<ref name="veiga2">{{Cite news|author=Veiga, Abel|title=Former Sao Tome leader poised to retake power|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jK8Sr91mcGhbjwFdA3PWQXAXM8ZQ?docId=CNG.5535797e6ca750ddf38512858f6cc18f.621|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140301004613/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jK8Sr91mcGhbjwFdA3PWQXAXM8ZQ?docId=CNG.5535797e6ca750ddf38512858f6cc18f.621|url-status=dead|archive-date=1 March 2014|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Google News|access-date=8 August 2011}}</ref> A run-off to be contested between Pinto da Costa and Carvalho was announced on the same day.<ref name="veiga1"/> Pinto da Costa received the backing of the majority of eliminated candidates,<ref name="veiga2"/> and he was expected to win comfortably.<ref name="preresult">{{Cite news|author=Staff writers|title=Sao Tome picks new president|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5h52_Ja8nGpjuYBH8s7UFlAGOqAwg?docId=CNG.5461b9ba016d10bd037873b0126a788c.861|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120527092803/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5h52_Ja8nGpjuYBH8s7UFlAGOqAwg?docId=CNG.5461b9ba016d10bd037873b0126a788c.861|url-status=dead|archive-date=27 May 2012|date=7 August 2011|newspaper=Google News|agency=Agence France-Presse|access-date=7 August 2011}}</ref>

Pinto da Costa won the runoff, held 7 August, by five percentage points.<ref name="publico"/>

==Background==
{{Main|Politics of São Tomé and Príncipe}}
Manuel Pinto da Costa previously served as São Tomé and Príncipe's first president from independence in 1975. He governed the islands as a [[One-party state|one-party]] [[socialist state]] under the [[Movement for the Liberation of São Tomé and Príncipe]] ({{langx|pt|Movimento de Libertação de São Tomé e Príncipe}}, MLSTP).<ref name="preresult"/> In 1991, the [[Democratization|legalisation]] of opposition political parties led to the country's first [[1991 São Tomé and Príncipe presidential election|election]] under a democratic system. Pinto da Costa was not a candidate in that election and instead announced he would retire from politics. The MLSTP did not present an alternative candidate and [[Miguel Trovoada]] was elected unopposed. Despite his previous declaration, Pinto da Costa returned to participate in the [[1996 São Tomé and Príncipe presidential election|presidential elections of 1996]], but was narrowly defeated by Trovoada. In 2001, he ran against incumbent president Fradique de Menezes, and was again unsuccessful.<ref name="veiga1"/>

Pinto da Costa resigned from the MLSTP in 2005. The party is currently led by [[Aurélio Martins]], who placed sixth in the first round vote count. Other major candidates included former prime minister [[Maria das Neves]] and former defence minister Elsa Pinto, both independents. Pinto da Costa's main rival, Carvalho, represented the ADI, which won the parliamentary elections in August 2010 and is the ruling party of incumbent Prime Minister [[Patrice Trovoada]].<ref name="publico"/><ref name="preresult"/><ref>{{Cite news|title=Sao Tome votes in presidential poll|url=http://www.africasia.com/services/news_africa/article.php?ID=CNG.2b4f6fd7caf05132cb9f1b6338bac556.2d1|access-date=25 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120318105257/http://www.africasia.com/services/news_africa/article.php?ID=CNG.2b4f6fd7caf05132cb9f1b6338bac556.2d1|archive-date=18 March 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Conduct==
Missions from the [[African Union]], [[Community of Portuguese Language Countries]] and the [[Economic Community of Central African States]] sent observers to monitor the election, which was declared free and fair.<ref name="review"/> The only major controversy observed was a [[boycott]] by around 30,000 from five small villages on São Tomé's northern shore, in protest over grievances with living conditions that had not been addressed.<ref name="preresult"/><ref name="review"/> The polls were re-opened in these villages on 20 July, but the results did not affect the outcome.<ref name="review"/>

==Results==
A total of 92,639 citizens were registered to vote.<ref name="eldata">{{Cite web|title=Elections in São Tomé and Príncipe|url=http://africanelections.tripod.com/st.html#2011_Presidential_Election|work=African Elections Database|access-date=8 August 2011}}</ref> In the first round, the national electoral commission, headed by Victor Correia,<ref name="publico"/> recorded a turnout of 68%.<ref name="eldata"/><ref name="review">{{Cite web|title=African elections held in the past year|url=http://africanelections.tripod.com/recent.html|work=African Elections Database|access-date=28 July 2011}}</ref> Of the 120 candidates, Da Costa and Carvalho won the most votes (35.6% and 21.8% respectively), but neither candidate received enough support to claim a majority.<ref name="voa"/> Delfim Neves and Maria das Neves both won substantial vote counts (over 14% each), but only the first two placeholders went through to the run-off. After the results were confirmed, most of the eliminated candidates,<ref name="voa"/> including Delfim Neves, Maria das Neves and Aurélio Martins, endorsed da Costa's bid for the run-off.<ref>{{Cite web|title=São Tomé and Príncipe 2011|url=http://welections.wordpress.com/2011/07/21/sao-tome-and-principe-2011/|work=World Elections|access-date=23 July 2011}}</ref>

